I don't think the CGA was capable of anything higher than 80x25. If the
internal graphics mode is 640x200 (the max for CGA monitors, I think)
and an 8x8 font is used you get an 80x25 text mode. The EGA used 640x350
as its underlying graphics mode, but had an 8x14 font for
crisper-looking text at 80x25. You could still tell it to use the 8x8
font, which would result in an 80x43 text mode. The VGA was the same
except it used 640x400 with an 8x16 font, or 80x50 with the 8x8 font.

To make this slightly more on topic, I have an old Hercules monochrome
card in the system I play Nethack on (although I usually SSH in from a
system that has colour :-). This had a 720x348 graphics mode so could do
90x43 if you used the 8x8 font (the Linux framebuffer does this, as well
as a DOS-based text editor called SLED).

